    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the hierarchy for dermatological coding on a few recent claims. Specifically, we're seeing atypical presentations of localized skin discoloration and irregular texture patterns that don't clearly fit into the standard L-series categories for dermatitis. How should we approach the hierarchy when the morphology is irregular and doesn't match a standard diagnostic pattern? Should we be looking at more general unspecified codes or is there a specific way to sequence these when the physical characteristics are non-standard?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When morphology is irregular and doesn't fit standard patterns, you should generally exclude the diagnosis from reimbursement until a more definitive code can be assigned. If the presentation remains undocumented by the physician, healthcare providers often use unspecified codes as a fallback, but this should be avoided whenever possible even for non-standard cases. It is crucial to ensure that any code selected accurately reflects both the location and the specific clinical findings to avoid reporting failures or fraudulent billing claims.",
      "source": "target",
