    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but we're seeing cases where the texture itself is what's causing the hesitation. For example, we have some patients with these highly irregular, pitted, or scaly dermatological anomalies that almost look like they've been externally compromised or have an unusual/bizarre surface pattern. If the physician's notes describe the skin as \"mottled,\" \"pitted,\" or \"deeply textured\" in a way that doesn't align with common inflammatory or neoplastic descriptions, how do we differentiate that in the code hierarchy? Is there a way to capture the specific \"atypical\" nature of the appearance without just defaulting to an unspecified code?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When specific descriptors like \"mottled\" or \"pitted\" appear non-standard, begin by verifying if there is an underlying condition causing these changes, such as localized collagen disorder or lichen simplex chronicus with secondary atrophy. ICD-10-CM Alphabetic Index and Tabular List often allow you to navigate these descriptions to specific subcategorizations based on mechanisms like \"change in skin texture\" or \"atrophy\" rather than defaulting immediately to general unspecified codes. If no matchable code exists after exhaustive searching, an unspecified code may be the only legally compliant option, though documenting the patient's full description in the medical record is vital for quality tracking.",
      "source": "target",
